), solves problems extremely well (technical), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), but is regarded as a know-it-all (cooperation/communication) and has a hot temper (crew cooperation). Also no explicit reliability note for D, but responsibility and problem solving suggest reliability but not explicit.\n\nWe need to contribute: maybe provide observations on B and D regarding reliability and stress resilience, to address the gap. Also evaluate them relative to C. Then state current recommendation: maybe still C, but we might consider B if stress resilience strong, but B has crew cooperation issues. D has responsibility but hot temper.\n\nThus we can say: I can provide that B is very conscientious (reliable) and handles stress well (stress resilience). D responds adequately to unexpected events (stress resilience) but we lack direct reliability evidence. Then argue that while B's reliability and stress resilience are strong, his nasty remarks raise crew cooperation concerns, making C still preferable despite communication issues. Or we could shift to B if we think crew cooperation can be mitigated. But likely we keep C.\n\nThus public message: agree with Agent 3 that C remains provisional lead, add B's reliability and stress resilience evidence, note D's stress resilience but concerns, and conclude still C.\n\nVote: Candidate C.\n\nNow produce output."}
